  "answer_text": "First, a quick clarification: while people often use the term \"lazy eye\" (amblyopia) to describe eyes that point in different directions, the medical term for misaligned eyes is actually **strabismus**. \n\nWhen speaking with someone who has strabismus, you might feel unsure of where to look. To make the interaction comfortable, respectful, and natural for both of you, follow these practical guidelines:\n\n### 1. Focus on the eye that is looking at you\nMost people with strabismus have a \"dominant\" eye that they use to focus on you, while the other eye may wander slightly inward, outward, upward, or downward. If you look directly at their dominant eye (the one making direct contact with yours), the interaction will feel completely normal and comfortable for both of you. \n\n### 2. Look at the bridge of their nose\nIf you find yourself getting distracted, or if you aren't sure which eye is the dominant one, look at the bridge of their nose (right between their eyes). This is a classic social trick: to the other person, it looks exactly like normal, direct eye contact, but it relieves the pressure on you to choose an eye.\n\n### 3. Avoid \"gaze-darting\" and looking behind you\nPeople with strabismus are usually highly aware of their condition. \n* **Avoid darting your eyes** back and forth between their two eyes, as they will easily notice your eyes jumping around and may feel self-conscious.\n* **Never look behind you** or to the side to see what their other eye is \"looking at.\" Rest assured, they are looking at you and paying full attention.\n\n### 4. Use normal, relaxed eye contact rules\nNo one maintains intense, unbroken eye contact in a casual conversation. It is entirely normal to look away briefly while you are thinking, speaking, or gesturing, and then look back when they speak. Let your gaze be relaxed and natural rather than a stiff, hyper-focused stare.\n\n### 5. Prioritize the conversation over the eyes\nAt the end of the day, people with strabismus just want to be treated like anyone else. If you focus fully on what they are *saying* rather than how they are *looking*, your body language will naturally relax, and any awkwardness will fade away.",
